  "summary": "Results inbox worked under RBC-INBOX-01, RBC-RAD-05 and RBC-LAB-03. 8 original items plus 5 that arrived while I worked: 12 closed, 1 left open on purpose. I confirmed each patient by two identifiers.\n\nOriginal items:\n- Raj Patel (pt-0006), CT urogram: no cause found for the hematuria. It showed a 4 mm LLL nodule that is already followed in lung screening. I first ordered the next screening LDCT for Mar–May 2027, with a tracking task, a patient message and a note. This was later replaced (see below).\n- Edra Ondricka (pt-0016), TSH: normal; closed. Potassium: the lab corrected the first-reported 6.3 to 4.4 (analyzer error), which is normal. No action, closed.\n- Leslie Parisian (pt-0007), CTA preliminary read: the final read (dr-00084) shows no PE and no nodule (the \"nodule\" was a vessel). Closed: no follow-up needed under RBC-RAD-05.\n- Leslie Parisian (pt-0007), BMP: kidney injury (creatinine 0.98 to 1.64 in about 35 h, after IV contrast). Urgent repeat BMP ordered by 10/26, urgent patient message, note.\n- Helen Marchetti (pt-0064), ED creatinine 1.36: no creatinine in the prior 7 days to compare with, so it doesn't meet the kidney-injury rule. Closed.\n- Carol Whitfield (pt-0033), creatinine 1.31: I left out the 10/21 value because the lab withdrew it as entered in error. 1.31 doesn't meet the rule against 1.27 on 10/17. Closed.\n- Walter Brennan (pt-0008), potassium 6.4 flagged hemolyzed: I first followed the hemolyzed-sample rule: urgent repeat BMP by 10/26 (sr-t0015), patient message, note.\n\nArrivals, and decisions I changed:\n- Walter Brennan (pt-0008): the lab corrected the result — the hemolysis comment belonged to another patient's specimen, so 6.4 is real. I switched to the non-hemolyzed rule:\n  - On-call pool notified at stat priority.\n  - Stat BMP ordered for today (sr-t0019).\n  - Corrected stat message to the patient: come in today, with ED warning signs.\n  - Medication review: no potassium-raising drug was safe to hold. Metoprolol is continued, since stopping it suddenly is unsafe with his heart disease.\n  - Note written. **Cancel sr-t0015 as a duplicate** — I have no tool to cancel lab or imaging orders.\n- Raj Patel (pt-0006): an addendum to his 4/14 screening CT found a new 5 mm RUL nodule, now Lung-RADS 3, with a 6-month LDCT advised.\n  - I ordered an LDCT due by 11/14/2026 (sr-t0029) with a tracking task.\n  - He has a corrected message, and a note is written.\n  - **The 2027 LDCT order (sr-t0001) and its tracking task (task-t0002) are now superseded and need withdrawing.** I couldn't cancel either, so I left you a task (task-t0031) to do it.\n- Carol Whitfield (pt-0033): an urgent care creatinine of 1.78, 9 h after the 1.31, is a kidney injury. Urgent repeat BMP ordered by 10/26, urgent patient message, note. Hydrochlorothiazide was started 10/17 and is worth reviewing. My earlier decision on the 1.31 stands.\n- Helen Marchetti (pt-0064): an urgent care creatinine of 1.48 has nothing to compare with in the prior 7 days. No action, closed.\n- Leslie Parisian (pt-0007), chest X-ray (inbox-a0002): **left open.** Only a preliminary read exists (possible 7 mm RUL opacity, suggests considering CT). No final has been issued; act once it is.\n\nYour messages queue still has 6 refill requests that were there before I started. I didn't touch them, since you asked for the results inbox only.",
